          "distinct": true,
          "id": "4f146c311e1e6c5683953e189b7c5592ec651b72",
          "message": "fix: decline JoinScan pushdown for cross-scale NUMERIC join keys (#6100) (#6352)\n\n## Summary\nFixes #6100.\n\nNUMERIC fast fields with precision <= 18 are represented internally as\n`Numeric64`, a scaled `Int64` (e.g. `numeric(10,2)` value `1.10` is\nstored as raw integer `110`). `validate_and_build_clause` only confirmed\nboth sides of a join key resolved to an indexed columnar fast field \u2014 it\nnever compared their scale. JoinScan would build a DataFusion\n`HashJoinExec` comparing the two sides' *raw, unscaled* Int64 values, so\n`numeric(10,2)` vs `numeric(10,3)` (both logically equal) compared `110`\nagainst `1100` and always returned false, silently dropping valid join\nmatches.\n\n**Fix**: `numeric_pushdown_safe()` now allows JoinScan pushdown only\nwhen:\n1. Both sides are `Numeric64` with equal scale (the core #6100 fix), or\n2. Both sides are `NumericBytes` (precision > 18) with\n`numeric_bytes_layouts_differ` (existing check) confirming compatible\nbyte layout \u2014 this restores a previously-working case that an earlier,\nover-conservative version of this fix had accidentally declined.\n\nAnything else (mismatched types, unresolvable scale) declines the\npushdown, falling back to Postgres's native join executor, which is\nalways correct regardless of scale.\n\n**Known, documented, out-of-scope gap**: `AggregateScan`'s own join-key\npushdown has the identical unscaled-Int64 comparison bug \u2014 not fixed\nhere since it's a separate code path and this issue is scoped to\nJoinScan. The new test file explicitly disables\n`paradedb.enable_aggregate_custom_scan` for this reason.\n\n## Test plan\n- [x] 6 regression scenarios in `joinscan_numeric_scale_mismatch.sql`:\ncross-scale Numeric64 (the original bug), same-scale sanity control,\nunbounded NUMERIC (unresolvable scale), Numeric64-vs-NumericBytes type\nmismatch, and same-scale-compatible-layout NumericBytes-vs-NumericBytes\n(confirms pushdown is restored, not just always declined).\n- [x] `cargo pgrx regress pg16 joinscan_numeric_scale_mismatch` passes\n(2/2, no diffs).\n- [x] Verified via `EXPLAIN (COSTS OFF)` that the\nNumericBytes-vs-NumericBytes case genuinely re-engages `Custom Scan\n(ParadeDB Join Scan)`, not just falling back everywhere.\n\n\ud83e\udd16 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
