          "distinct": true,
          "id": "624d4be700ce40e2083827daeaa82d9e4434620b",
          "message": "fix: prevent double-conversion of range partition bounds for NUMERIC fields (#6104) (#6373)\n\n## Ticket(s) Closed\n\n- Closes #6104\n\n## What\n\nRange-partitioned joins on NUMERIC fields were treating partition\nboundaries that were already encoded in the index\u2019s stored\nrepresentation as ordinary user-input values.\n\nThis caused two distinct problems:\n\n- `Numeric64` boundaries were scaled a second time. For example, `1.23`,\nalready stored as `I64(123)` for a scale-2 field, could be converted to\n`12300`, skewing the executed partitions.\n- `NumericBytes` boundaries were already encoded as sortable bytes and\ncould not pass through the user-input NUMERIC conversion path correctly.\n\nSplit points also cross JSON boundaries while being dispatched to\nworkers. The default `PdbOwnedValue` serialization could change positive\n`I64` values into `U64` values and byte values into strings, so their\nexact stored representation was not guaranteed to survive transport.\n\n## Why\n\n`Query::Range` accepts logical/user-form values and converts them into\nthe representation used by the index.\n\nRange partition boundaries are different: they are sampled from the\nindex and are already in stored form. Applying the user-input conversion\nto them again changes their meaning or makes them invalid.\n\n## How\n\n- Added `Query::StoredRange` for range bounds that are already in stored\nform.\n- Refactored range-query construction so `Range` and `StoredRange` share\nfinal Tantivy query construction while only `Range` performs user-input\nconversion.\n- Updated range partition pruning to emit `StoredRange`.\n- Added exact element-wise serialization for `RangeSplitPoints::points`,\npreserving `I64` and `Bytes` values across worker transport.\n- Added `BinaryView + PdbOwnedValue::Bytes` conversion so `NumericBytes`\nsplit points can be represented in DataFusion range partitioning.\n- Added the `StoredRange` variant to base-scan predicate detection.\n- Added a stability changelog entry.\n\n## Tests\n\n- Added real-index execution coverage for `Numeric64` and `NumericBytes`\npartition bounds, asserting the exact document IDs returned from the\nmiddle partition.\n- Added a JSON round-trip test confirming that `RangeSplitPoints`\npreserves exact `I64` and `Bytes` values.\n- Added DataFusion conversion coverage for `NumericBytes`/`BinaryView`\nsplit points.\n- Added SQL regression coverage comparing serial and MPP execution\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Mohammad Dashti <mdashti@gmail.com>",
